From 1037341ae93dce29b5ac0cfab4aa15c2865772cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric Yang <eric.yang.wiwynn@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Apr 2026 04:20:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add drive state manager daemon
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add phosphor-drive-state-manager, a single-instance daemon that
implements the xyz.openbmc_project.State.Drive D-Bus interface for
individual drive power control. The daemon discovers drives at runtime
through entity-manager, creating per-device state objects dynamically
within a single process.

The daemon maps RequestedDriveTransition to systemd targets and monitors
JobRemoved signals to update CurrentDriveState. Supported transitions
are On, Off, Reboot, HardReboot, and Powercycle. Seven systemd target
templates are included for transition, state, and marker operations.
Platform-specific hardware operations attach to these targets via
.wants/ directories, keeping all hardware logic out of the state
manager.

State transitions cross-validate JobRemoved signals against the target
active state to prevent transient state flips when systemd stops
conflicting targets during power transitions.

Each state object publishes a D-Bus association to its inventory path
for bmcweb state object discovery. Drives associated with a host via
Inventory.Decorator.ManagedHost receive a host-dependency check that
rejects power-on transitions with a D-Bus NotAllowed error when the
host is off. Drives also follow host power lifecycle: host-off triggers
drive poweroff, host-on triggers drive poweron.

Motivation:
On storage-dense platforms, individual NVMe/SSD drives need to be power
cycled or reset from the BMC independently of chassis or host state.
The State.Drive interface exists in phosphor-dbus-interfaces [1] but
had no upstream consumer.

Tested:
On a Yosemite5 platform with entity-manager exposing
Item.Drive inventory objects (ssd0, ssd1) via JSON configuration
(simulated drives, no real NVMe power hardware attached to targets):

  busctl tree xyz.openbmc_project.State.Drive
  └─ /xyz/openbmc_project/state/drive/ssd0
  └─ /xyz/openbmc_project/state/drive/ssd1

  busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.State.Drive \
    /xyz/openbmc_project/state/drive/ssd0 \
    xyz.openbmc_project.State.Drive RequestedDriveTransition \
    s xyz.openbmc_project.State.Drive.Transition.Off

  CurrentDriveState transitions: Unknown → NotReady → Offline.
  Transition.On restores to Ready.

All five transition types (On, Off, Reboot, HardReboot, Powercycle)
execute the corresponding systemd target and reach the expected final
state.

Host power-off (busctl set RequestedHostTransition Off) triggers
automatic drive poweroff for all associated drives. Both ssd0 and ssd1
transition to Offline. Host power-on restores all drives to Ready.
Power-on request while host is off is rejected with NotAllowed:

  busctl set-property ... Transition.On
  Failed to set property: The operation is not allowed

Reference:
[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/+/43155
[2] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/+/89402
[3] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/+/90669

